   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to requiring\nsupermarkets to make surplus food available to qualifying charities",
    "basePrintNo": "S8884",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8884-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 36,
       "fullName": "Jamaal Bailey",
       "imgName": "924_Jamaal_Bailey.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1102,
       "sessionMemberId": 1098,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "BAILEY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 17-C §§223 - 223-e, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8884\n\nSPONSOR: ALCANTARA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in\nrelation to requiring supermarkets to make surplus food available to\nqualifying charities\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation is designed to increase food donations to food banks\nand other providers who feed the needy and to help reduce solid food\nwaste in landfills which create dangerous emissions.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the agricultural and markets law by adding\na new article 16-C to establish the \"Surplus Food to Charitable Organ-\nizations Act.\" The Act provides the following:\n\n1. The legislative intent.\n\n2. Definitions as used in this article, including:\n\n(a) \"Excess food\" means edible food that is not sold or used by a super-\nmarket;\n\n(b) \"Qualifying charities\" means a religious, charitable or not-for-pro-\nfit organization which provides food at no cost to the poor, needy,\ndisadvantaged or at-risk persons, including a food pantry, soup kitchen\nor community based organization that provides food at no costs to such\npersons; and\n\n(c) \"Supermarket\" means a retail store at a given location which has\nmore than ten thousand square feet devoted to the sale of food and\ngroceries for human consumption.\n\n3. Availability requirement.\n\n(a) Every supermarket shall make a reasonable effort to make available a\nqualifying charity, surplus food which the supermarket from time to time\nhas in its possession.\n\n(b) No supermarket shall be required to make available a particular\nquantity or level of surplus food or to transport or distribute any\nsurplus food in connection with this article.\n\n(c) A supermarket may, in accordance with any applicable laws, dispose\nof surplus food which is not timely collected by a qualifying charity.\n\n(d) A supermarket shall be deemed in compliance with this section if, in\ngood faith, it arranges with one or more qualifying charities that have\nrequested in writing to collect surplus food for the collection of such\nsurplus food.\n\n(e) A supermarket may impose restrictions to ensure that the retrieval\nof surplus food does not interfere with its business operations.\n\n4. Exclusions.\n\n(a) Surplus food shall not include fresh milk, fresh meat, fish or poul-\ntry; food damaged by storage conditions; pests, mold, bacteria or other\ncontamination; food offered for sale from a hot, cold or prepared food\nbar; food subject to recall; food returned to a supplier; food donated\nto a qualifying charity; food sold to a food remarketer or restaurant or\nother preparer of food for human consumption or sold to a farmer or\nother producer.\n\n(b) Supermarkets shall not include, hotels, motels, restaurants and\ncafeterias, bakeries, caterers, hospitals, assisted and independent\nliving facilities, nursing homes, hospices, group homes, drug stores,\neducational institutions, food courts in shopping malls, food retailers\nat airports or other transportation facilities, gas stations, sports\narenas, or movie theaters or any other similar establishments.\n\n4. Immunity from liability. No supermarket, qualifying charity, or any\nemployee, officer, shareholder, partner, agent, volunteer or religious\nleader thereof who provides, makes available, distributes or otherwise\nfacilitates the distribution of surplus food as provided in this arti-\ncle, shall be liable to any person for damages for injuries or death\nalleged to have been sustained as a result of the condition of such\nsurplus food, unless caused by the gross negligence or intentional\nconduct of such entity or individual.\n\n5. Construction. Nothing in this article shall supersede any state or\nfederal health laws or regulations regarding the handling of foods.\n\nSection 2 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nHunger in the U.S. and our state is a serious problem. An estimated 50\nmillion Americans, including nearly 16 million children, do not have\nsufficient food. Meanwhile, 60 - 100 million tons of edible food is\ndiscarded in the United States, primarily to solid waste landfills.\nDecomposition of organic waste accounts for over 15 percent of our\nnation's emissions of methane, a potent greenhouse gas.\n\nHunger and food insecurity - the uncertainty of having, or inability to\nacquire, enough food to meet household food needs exists in every county\nin our state. An estimated 2.8 million New Yorkers (over 14%) are facing\nhunger and food insecurity. Moreover, a significant number of those\nhouseholds do not qualify for federal nutrition assistance.\n\nAs noted in the Food Bank for Westchester Food Dating Guide, the majori-\nty of food items that are close to or past the indicated date on their\npackages are still safe for consumption. This includes items passed the\n\"expiration date,\" \"sell by date,\" \"use by date\" and \"pack date.\"  These\ncodes are used by manufacturers to ensure that consumers receive their\nproduct in peak quality. Supermarkets regularly dispose of these unused\nfood items and most are of good quality and safe to eat. This legis-\nlation will provide a process for qualifying charities to make arrange-\nments to collect surplus food from supermarkets in order to provide this\nfood for free to the needy.\n\nWhile supermarkets of a certain size are required to participate, they\nare not required to provide a particular quantity of food or transport\nor distribute it. Qualifying organizations must make a written request\nfor the surplus food and arrange to collect the food at a date and time\narranged with the supermarket. Not only will there be no cost to partic-\nipating supermarkets - there may be savings - since supermarkets will no\nlonger have to pay to have surplus food collected and discarded.\n\nFinally, supermarkets, qualifying charities, their employees and others\nwho make available or otherwise distribute surplus food under this law,\nare protected from liability for any injuries alleged to be sustained by\na person with respect to the condition of the surplus food unless caused\nby the gross negligence or intentional conduct of such entity or indi-\nvidual.\n\nMaking excess food available to qualifying charities that feed the\nhungry will go a long way to help address food insecurity in our state\nwithout additional costs to participating supermarkets. In addition, the\ndonation of surplus food will also reduce food waste disposed of in\nsolid waste landfills which create dangerous emissions.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become law. Effective immediately the addition, amendment\nand/or repeal of any rule and regulation necessary for the implementa-\ntion of this act on its effective date are authorized to be made on or\nbefore such date.\n",
